% 48f-self-hosting-io.weft — Stage 8g: the buffered output layer, written in C,
% compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: Buffered Output in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-io}

The last of the foundational subsystems to port is the one every other layer
ultimately writes through: the buffered output of Chapter~\ref{ch:io}.  Every
diagnostic, every \texttt{-{}-dump} listing, and the assembly the \texttt{.s}
back end emits pass through \texttt{kcc\_io\_puts}, \texttt{kcc\_io\_write}, and
their siblings.  Unlike the earlier ports this one owns real \emph{mutable
global state}---a 4\,KiB buffer, a write cursor, and a file descriptor---so it
is also a test that \kcc{} initialises a non-\texttt{main} unit's file-scope
globals correctly: \texttt{kcc\_io\_fd} starts at~1 (stdout) and
\texttt{kcc\_io\_cap} at~4096, values a module initialiser must set before the
first byte is written.

\section{State and the Flush Contract}

The module keeps four globals under the exact names and initial values the
assembly used: the destination \texttt{kcc\_io\_fd} (1, stdout), the current
\texttt{kcc\_io\_pos} (0), the capacity \texttt{kcc\_io\_cap} (4096), and the
4\,KiB \texttt{kcc\_io\_buf} itself.  \texttt{kcc\_io\_flush} writes the buffered
prefix through the \texttt{kcc\_sys\_write} syscall wrapper and resets the
cursor; the two syscall-facing routines (\texttt{flush} and the unbuffered
\texttt{kcc\_io\_err}) are the module's only dependencies, declared
\texttt{extern} along with \texttt{kcc\_strlen}.

The one behavioural subtlety a faithful port must preserve is \emph{when} the
flush happens: \texttt{kcc\_io\_putc} flushes \emph{before} storing when the
buffer is already full, whereas \texttt{kcc\_io\_puts} and \texttt{kcc\_io\_write}
store first and flush once the cursor reaches the end.  Both avoid overrun, but
the resulting flush boundaries differ, so the C reproduces each shape exactly.

@l c
@d Selfhost: io preamble @{
long kcc_sys_write(long fd, const void *buf, unsigned long n);
unsigned long kcc_strlen(const char *s);

long kcc_io_fd = 1;
unsigned long kcc_io_pos = 0;
unsigned long kcc_io_cap = 4096;
char kcc_io_buf[4096];
@}

\section{Flush and Put}

@l c
@d Selfhost: io flush @{
void kcc_io_flush(void) {
    if (kcc_io_pos == 0) return;
    kcc_sys_write(kcc_io_fd, kcc_io_buf, kcc_io_pos);
    kcc_io_pos = 0;
}
@}

@l c
@d Selfhost: io putc @{
void kcc_io_putc(int c) {
    if (kcc_io_pos >= kcc_io_cap) kcc_io_flush();
    kcc_io_buf[kcc_io_pos] = (char)c;
    kcc_io_pos = kcc_io_pos + 1;
}
@}

@l c
@d Selfhost: io puts @{
void kcc_io_puts(const char *s) {
    while (*s) {
        kcc_io_buf[kcc_io_pos] = *s;
        kcc_io_pos = kcc_io_pos + 1;
        if (kcc_io_pos >= 4096) kcc_io_flush();
        s = s + 1;
    }
}
@}

\section{Write, Format, and Error}

\texttt{kcc\_io\_write} emits an explicit byte count (for token text, which is a
pointer/length pair rather than a NUL-terminated string).  \texttt{kcc\_io\_putu}
renders an unsigned integer by taking decimal digits least-significant first
into a small stack buffer and writing them in order.  \texttt{kcc\_io\_err}
sends a string straight to stderr through the syscall, unbuffered, so a
diagnostic survives an immediate crash.

@l c
@d Selfhost: io write @{
void kcc_io_write(const void *ptr, unsigned long len) {
    const char *p = (const char *)ptr;
    unsigned long i = 0;
    while (i < len) {
        kcc_io_buf[kcc_io_pos] = p[i];
        kcc_io_pos = kcc_io_pos + 1;
        if (kcc_io_pos >= 4096) kcc_io_flush();
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
@}

@l c
@d Selfhost: io putu @{
void kcc_io_putu(unsigned long n) {
    char buf[24];
    int i = 24;
    if (n == 0) {
        i = i - 1;
        buf[i] = '0';
    } else {
        while (n) {
            i = i - 1;
            buf[i] = (char)('0' + (n % 10));
            n = n / 10;
        }
    }
    kcc_io_write(&buf[i], (unsigned long)(24 - i));
}
@}

@l c
@d Selfhost: io err @{
void kcc_io_err(const char *s) {
    unsigned long len = kcc_strlen(s);
    kcc_sys_write(2, s, len);
}
@}

\section{Assembling the Translation Unit}

@l c
@d Selfhost: io @{
@<Selfhost: io preamble@>
@<Selfhost: io flush@>
@<Selfhost: io putc@>
@<Selfhost: io puts@>
@<Selfhost: io write@>
@<Selfhost: io putu@>
@<Selfhost: io err@>
@}

\section{Verification}
\label{sec:selfhost-io-verify}

\texttt{make selfhost-io} runs the drop-in loop: compile
\texttt{gen/selfhost/io.c} with the assembly-built \kcc{}, relink a second
compiler with the hand-written \texttt{io.o} replaced by the kcc-compiled
\texttt{io\_c.o}, and re-run the code-generation suite.  Because the suite's
harness reads the exit status rather than the text, and because the compiler
itself writes nothing on a clean run, this port is also implicitly checked by
every other verification that now flows through the C buffer.

@d Selfhost: io verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
BASE="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
# make selfhost-io — compile kcc's buffered output in C with kcc, relink,
# run the suite.
set -euo pipefail

SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)
SH=$BASE/selfhost
sources="main syscalls arena strings io hashtab vec strtab source lexer \
lexer_tables diag preprocessor parser expressions statements declarations \
types symtab conversions sema ir opt emit_arm64 emit_x86 linker codegen emit_macho dwarf driver"

echo "[1/3] compiling $BASE/selfhost/io.c with kcc"
$BASE/kcc -c "$SH/io.c" -o "$SH/io_c.o" >/dev/null

echo "[2/3] linking $BASE/kcc_selfhost_io (io.o -> kcc-compiled io_c.o)"
OBJ=""
for f in $sources; do
    if [ "$f" = "io" ]; then OBJ="$OBJ $SH/io_c.o"; else OBJ="$OBJ $BASE/src/${f}.o"; fi
done
ld -o $BASE/kcc_selfhost_io $OBJ -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64

echo "[3/3] running the codegen suite with the self-hosted output layer"
cp $BASE/kcc "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_io"
cp $BASE/kcc_selfhost_io $BASE/kcc
set +e
bash $BASE/tests/test_codegen.sh
status=$?
cp "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_io" $BASE/kcc
exit $status
@}
